1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,042
Pulse attributes. One derived value for every record that survives the filters, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:07,842 --> 00:00:16,917
Attributes run third, after the filters, so they only see the records that survived. Groups, aggregations and windows can all read the new column.

3
00:00:17,717 --> 00:00:23,058
Each attribute names a type, a source and a label. The label becomes the new column.

4
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:32,741
The label must be new. Reusing a field name, a feature column or an earlier label is refused, and predict catches it before anything runs.

5
00:00:33,541 --> 00:00:40,376
Features add columns before filters, for every record. Attributes add them after, for the survivors only.

6
00:00:41,176 --> 00:00:46,901
Row-local attributes read only the record in front of them, so they finish in one pass and stream.

7
00:00:47,701 --> 00:00:57,160
Two-pass attributes score a row against the whole column or a model. Pulse makes one pass to learn the statistics, then a second to score every row.

8
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:07,035
Percentile rank is the exception. It has to sort the values first, so it can't stream. All of these statistics come from the filtered rows only.

9
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:17,059
A formula evaluates an expression for each row. Arithmetic on a null raises an error that stops the whole request, so guard it with a default.

10
00:01:17,859 --> 00:01:24,011
Categories read as their labels and sets read as lists. True becomes one and false becomes zero.

11
00:01:24,811 --> 00:01:32,286
A formula can also call total, which sums an expression over every filtered row and hands the same figure to each one.

12
00:01:33,086 --> 00:01:36,721
That makes shares easy: each income divided by the total.

13
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:45,337
A formula with total is two-pass, and total is only allowed in formula attributes, never in a filter or an overlay.

14
00:01:46,137 --> 00:01:54,956
Attributes run in the order you list them, and a later one can read an earlier one's label: a formula, a z-score of it, then a formula of that.

15
00:01:55,756 --> 00:02:02,548
Pulse plans the passes for you. Each layer of two-pass attributes that depend on each other adds one more scan.

16
00:02:03,348 --> 00:02:12,636
A few attributes read source columns only, like code-in and the multivariate scores. To chain across requests, use Compose or ProcessChain.

17
00:02:13,436 --> 00:02:22,553
Z-score measures how many standard deviations a value sits from the mean, using the population mean and standard deviation of the filtered rows.

18
00:02:23,353 --> 00:02:29,142
T-score is the same idea on a friendlier scale: mean fifty, standard deviation ten.

19
00:02:29,942 --> 00:02:39,145
Normalized squeezes values into zero to one. Watch the outlier: one income of one hundred and forty pushes everyone else toward the bottom.

20
00:02:39,945 --> 00:02:44,838
Percentile gives each value its rank as a percentage. Ties never share a rank.

21
00:02:45,638 --> 00:02:52,259
And notice member ten, who has no income. These scores don't return null. They return zero, or fifty.

22
00:02:53,059 --> 00:03:01,408
Code-in flags whether a value is one of a set of codes, like the top two boxes of a rating scale. Its mean is the top-box share.

23
00:03:02,208 --> 00:03:09,384
A null counts as no. And if you filter to the codes first, the base shrinks, and every share reads one hundred percent.

24
00:03:10,184 --> 00:03:17,808
Date part pulls out one calendar part, such as the year and month. Set-has and popcount read multi-select answers.

25
00:03:18,608 --> 00:03:25,976
Regression attributes fit a least-squares model and give every row a diagnostic. Fitted is the model's prediction.

26
00:03:26,776 --> 00:03:34,101
Residual is the gap between the actual value and the prediction. Large residuals flag unusual responses.

27
00:03:34,901 --> 00:03:44,744
Leverage flags unusual predictor values, which pull hardest on the fit. Each slot fits its own model, and a null predictor gives null, never zero.

28
00:03:45,544 --> 00:03:53,211
Mahalanobis distance measures how far a row sits from the centre of several fields at once, allowing for how they move together.

29
00:03:54,011 --> 00:04:01,038
This member is tall but light. Each value looks ordinary on its own, but together they sit far outside the cloud.

30
00:04:01,838 --> 00:04:09,803
Principal-component and factor scores place each row on a fitted dimension. All three fit across the whole cohort, never per group.

31
00:04:10,603 --> 00:04:18,739
Here's what a null input becomes. Many attributes return a neutral number, while regression and multivariate scores return null.

32
00:04:19,539 --> 00:04:25,606
A neutral number gets averaged like any other value, so check this before you aggregate an attribute.

33
00:04:26,406 --> 00:04:34,286
That's the attribute toolkit today: formulas, standard scores, recodes, regression diagnostics and multivariate scores.

34
00:04:35,086 --> 00:04:42,902
More attributes are added over time, and embedders can register their own. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

35
00:04:43,702 --> 00:04:48,254
Remember: check what a null becomes before you average it. Next up, groupers.

36
00:04:49,054 --> 00:04:50,470
Pulse attributes.
